            "sessions": [
              { "id": "20261019-0940-p2dk", "title": "Payment dates on the schedule are a day early", "started": "Today, 09:40", "lastLine": "Fix it on a branch and I’ll make the change." }
            ],
            "session": { "id": "20261019-0940-p2dk", "title": "Payment dates on the schedule are a day early" },
            "state": { "kind": "idle" },
            "entries": [
              { "type": "user", "message": "Payment dates on the schedule are a day early in the app. Ana Morales’s loan is due on the 15th of each month, but the schedule lists the 14th: Nov 14, Dec 14, Jan 14. The design shows the 15th." },
              { "type": "tool", "name": "Read", "summary": "prelude/repayment-schedule/views/schedule/list.eta" },
              { "type": "tool", "name": "Grep", "summary": "dueDate in app/" },
              { "type": "tool", "name": "Read", "summary": "app/lib/dates.js" },
              { "type": "tool", "name": "Read", "summary": "app/views/schedule/list.eta" },
              { "type": "text", "html": "<p>The cause is in the app, not in the design. <code>formatDate</code> in <code>app/lib/dates.js</code> turns each due date (<code>2026-11-15</code>) into a <code>Date</code> at midnight UTC, then prints it in the server’s time zone, America/Chicago, where that moment is still the evening of the 14th.</p><p>The design’s states hold the dates as text and print them as they are, which is why its pages show the 15th. The Loan summary’s “Next payment” uses <code>formatDate</code> too, so it’s a day early as well.</p><p>What I’d change: format the date without a time zone in <code>formatDate</code>, and add a test with a date near midnight. Neither design needs to change. Fix it on a branch and I’ll make the change.</p>" },
              { "type": "turn", "ok": true }
            ],
            "rules": [],
            "questions": [],
            "decisions": [],
